.TH SAPHIRA-BUILD-MCP 1 "October 2026" "saphira-build-mcp 0.10.8" "Saphira"
.SH NAME
saphira-build-mcp \- Saphira build/package queries and evidence journal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira-build-mcp --mcp
.br
.B saphira-build-mcp --list-tools
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saphira-build-mcp
is a local MCP stdio server exposing Saphira package, build-queue,
artifact and Unix-identity state to agents, plus an Egg-local
evidence journal (package notes and policy records). It reads the
builder state: recipes, the APK and repository databases, the build
queue and retained logs. It never builds, signs, publishes,
installs or restarts anything, never executes recipe scripts, and
never opens a network socket. Its only write surface is its own
journal database; there is no recipe, queue, revision, signing,
publication or installation authority.
.PP
With
.B --mcp
it speaks JSON-RPC over stdin/stdout (newline-delimited objects;
LSP Content-Length framing accepted as fallback) and exits on EOF.
With
.B --list-tools
it prints the tools/list document (also the build-time self-check).
.SH TOOLS
Twenty-three tools. Seventeen are read-only: package_search,
package_info, package_dependencies, package_why_installed,
package_installed, package_build_status, package_build_history,
package_build_log, package_artifacts, build_queue,
package_identity, package_notes_get, package_policy_status,
package_readiness, checkpoint_resume, checkpoint_list,
total_recall. Six take
action with deliberately narrow authority: package_notes_add,
package_policy_record, checkpoint_save and checkpoint_close write
only to the journal; build_request queues through the daemon intake
(the daemon executes detached, the MCP never runs buildpkg);
package_bump runs bumppkg guards and all. None can sign, publish
or install: a staged row names the exact human-gated ask
(`sudo sign-apk-repo <pkg>`). Every result ends
with a [src: ...] provenance line naming the files read.
.PP
Checkpoints are a persistent register of unfinished work shared by
all agents on the host, and the initialize reply advertises the
active set (first five plus the total count) in its instructions
field. checkpoint_list shows the active register (newest first,
with the total, so a capped page never hides the backlog);
checkpoint_resume consumes one row by id, label, or newest;
checkpoint_close retires finished work by id without deleting its
history (a closed row stays resumable by id). A new agent lists
the register and asks the operator whether to resume one, start
fresh, or close one; it never auto-resumes.
.PP
Policy records pin the revisions they were verified against
(recipe, registry and artifact digests). A record whose pins no
longer match the live state reports STALE; absence of a record
reports UNVERIFIED. Stale evidence is never presented as valid.
.PP
Execution provenance: every checkpoint, note, policy record and
tool run can carry where it was written — hostname, username,
working directory, a stable caller-supplied session id, the agent
client, and whether that context came from the caller (client) or
was observed by the server (server). All six fields are optional
and nullable: unknown stays unknown, never fabricated, and
pre-provenance history reads back without them. Resume prints
the recorded working location alongside objective and next
action; recovery must never cd, execute, or assume a saved path
exists on another machine.
.PP
total_recall retrieves history the checkpoints summarise: ranked
full-text query, chronological timeline, or paginated export over
checkpoints, notes, policy evidence and tool-run metadata. Modes
take scope filters (session, package, label, project, author,
hostname, username, working directory), summaries by default with
full bodies only on request, and tiered budgets (compact 4 KiB,
standard 16 KiB, extended 64 KiB, deep 256 KiB) enforced before
anything is returned, with cursors for continuation. It never
runs automatically — checkpoint first, deeper recall only when
asked. What was never recorded cannot be retrieved: tool outputs
and conversation turns are not captured, and every response says
so.
.SH JOURNAL
The journal lives in notes.db (live default
/var/lib/saphira-build/notes.db on Egg) and is append-only:
new rows supersede, nothing is updated or deleted. Its tables are
maintained by this program. The database must never be carried in
/recipes except blank and empty.
.PP
Keeper and Homer are backup destinations only. Back up with a
SQLite-safe snapshot (sqlite3 notes.db .backup snapshot.db, which
uses the online backup API) and transfer the completed snapshot
with the existing backup infrastructure. Never copy the live file
while writes may occur, and never use Git for journal sync.
.PP
Journal attribution comes from $SBM_AUTHOR (or the per-call author
field, defaulting to "unspecified"): attribution, not
authentication. The recorder field is MCP clientInfo metadata:
client-reported and unverified.
.PP
Action tools require a reason (max 512 bytes) and journal it
automatically alongside the outcome: package_bump records why a
revision moved, build_request records why a build was queued.
build_request runs `saphira-build scan`, which is tree-wide by
daemon design but only queues genuinely changed work; the response
reports what moved for the requested package. package_bump
surfaces bumppkg guard refusals (never-published, 24h anti-loop,
disabled) as errors, never as silent success.
.SH EXTENSIONS
.BR /etc/saphira/mcp/tools.conf
declares an exposure allowlist for the built-ins and an extension
registry of curated external commands (SBM_TOOLS_CONF overrides the
path; a missing file means built-ins only). Grammar is strict and
fail-closed: a malformed or unsafe line disables extensions while
the built-ins stay fully on, and checkpoint_* tools can never be
switched off. Registered commands run with fixed argv via execve:
no shell exists anywhere on the path, callers pass no arguments,
and output is capped, timed, and audited to the journal tool_runs
table (tool, argv, agent, exit status, elapsed time). Class is read
or test; test entries run sandboxed by default (bwrap, SBM_BWRAP to
override).
.PP
Denylisted by shape: shells, interpreters, command-executing
filters, compilers, and anything that builds, signs, publishes or
installs, plus any path naming MCP-owned writable state. The
runner is an additional capability, never a second route around
build and package operations.
.SH CAPABILITIES
Three independent capabilities decide which tools are
registered. Gated tools vanish from tools/list and refuse calls
as unknown; the expose allowlist subtracts further but can never
re-enable a gated tool, and checkpoint_* can never be switched
off.
.PP
.B Journal
(always on): checkpoints, notes, policy records and status,
readiness, total_recall. Usable on any host with a journal,
independent of build execution.
.PP
.B Saphira state
(SBM_SAPHIRA=off gates it): all package_* readers,
package_identity and build_queue — everything that needs the
recipes tree, queue/repository/apk databases, accounts registry,
retained logs or incoming.
.PP
.B Build execution
(SBM_BUILD=off gates it): build_request and package_bump, the
only tools that change the world beyond the journal. Signing,
publication and installation have no tools at all and remain
explicit human gates. Unset or
.B on
keeps full behaviour; any other value is an explicit
configuration failure (exit status 2, nothing served).
.SH PORTABLE MODE
.B SBM_SAPHIRA=off
selects the journal capability without builder state: checkpoints,
notes, policy tools, readiness, total_recall and configured
externals keep working anywhere against the journal database
alone, so session handoff, recovery and lessons-learned travel
with the binary. Combine with
.B SBM_BUILD=off
to also refuse build and bump operations while keeping every
journal tool. The
expose allowlist keeps working inside all modes and still cannot
switch off checkpoint_*.
.PP
Portable hosts set
.B SBM_NOTES_DB
to a per-host, per-user journal path (recommended:
~/.local/share/saphira-build-mcp/notes.db, alongside
SBM_SAPHIRA=off in the launcher configuration). The builder
default (/var/lib/saphira-build/notes.db) is unchanged. No
mode-dependent path is ever invented: what is not explicitly
configured keeps the documented default. Journal tables are
created on first use, so a fresh path self-initialises; existing
journals stay readable across upgrades and across process
restarts, and historical rows are never migrated or deleted.
.SH WINDOWS
Windows x86-64 binaries are cross-compiled with MinGW-w64
(`make windows` for shared SQLite, `make windows-builtin` with
`SQLITE_SRC` pointing at the amalgamation). Standard input and
output run in binary mode so MCP framing survives; a trailing
carriage return on NDJSON input is tolerated. Subprocess
execution (external tools, sandboxed or not) is unavailable:
configured externals stay listed but every call fails closed.
Journal paths may use forward or back slashes; drive prefixes
(`C:`) are never created. Smoke-tested under Wine; native
Windows validation is still desirable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Data-source paths default to the live system and can be pointed at
fixtures for testing:
.B SBM_RECIPES SBM_QUEUE_DB SBM_REPO_DIR SBM_INCOMING SBM_TSV
.B SBM_PASSWD SBM_GROUP SBM_APKDB SBM_NOTES_DB SBM_TOOLS_CONF
.B SBM_BWRAP
.PP
.B SBM_AUTHOR
sets the default journal attribution (a per-call author field
overrides it; "unspecified" when neither is set).
.PP
.B SBM_HOSTNAME SBM_USERNAME SBM_CWD
override the server-observed hostname, username and working
directory recorded on journal writes. They exist for hermetic
tests; production writes carry live values unless the caller
supplied its own context.
.PP
.B SBM_SAPHIRA
selects builder mode ("on", the default when unset) or portable
mode ("off"); anything else fails before serving (see PORTABLE
MODE above).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (server exits 0 on stdin EOF). 1 on internal errors.
2 on usage errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR saphira-build (8)
.PP
Full integration documentation (protocol, per-tool reference with
curl and PowerShell forms, stdio clients, HTTP bridge, recovery,
Windows/Wine, tools.conf externals) lives in docs/ in the source
distribution and the project README.
